course: dictionary
lesson: 15452
title: Stifled: held back, stuffy, or stopped
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Emma gave a stifled giggle when the teacher turned round.» Emma was trying not to laugh out loud. Stifled means held back, so the giggle was quiet and hidden.
explain: «The tent was so hot and airless that breathing felt stifled.» Here stifled means stuffy. You can test it: swap in "stuffy" and the sentence still makes sense, because hot, stuffy air is hard to breathe.
explain: «Mia's ideas were stifled by strict rules.» Stifled means stopped from growing or being free. Look at the clue: strict rules are the reason her ideas could not grow.
fact: Stifled is the past form of the verb stifle, so it can describe something that has already been held back or stopped.
fact: Stifled means held back so it is not heard or seen, such as a stifled yawn or a stifled cry.
fact: Stifled can also mean unable to breathe well because the air is hot or stuffy.
fact: Stifling heat makes breathing hard, so never cover someone's nose and mouth: it can stop them breathing.
exercise: «She let out a stifled giggle when the teacher turned round.» What does stifled mean here? | Loud and wild | Broken into tiny pieces | *Held back so it could not be heard
exercise: «The tent was so hot and airless that breathing felt stifled.» What does stifled mean here? | *Hard to breathe because the air is stuffy | Cool and fresh | Covered in mud
exercise: «Mia's ideas were stifled by strict rules.» What does stifled mean here? | *Stopped from growing or being free | Read out loud | Shared with the whole class | Written down very neatly
test: «He stifled a cry when he stubbed his toe.» What does stifled mean here? | *Held back so it was not heard | Shouted very loudly | Covered with a cloth | Stuck in mud
test: «The smoky kitchen felt stifled, and the children coughed.» Which meaning fits best? | Full of bright light | *Hard to breathe because the air is hot or stuffy | Held back from speaking
exam: Asha says «stifled» always means quiet. Is she right? | No, it means very happy | *No, stifled can also mean stuffy air or stopped from growing | Yes, it always means quiet
